<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tioman Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tioman Island is a mountainous island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> off the east coast of Pahang, Malaysia. Its fresh water and sheltered anchorages made it a landmark and watering stop for ships sailing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca">Strait of Malacca</a> for many centuries. The French adventurer Mayrena, self-proclaimed king of the Sedang, died there in 1890.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tioman-island.jpg" alt="Tioman Island" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Gronemann from Switzerland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tioman_island_(3679435126).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tioman Island, in Malay Pulau Tioman, lies 39 kilometres off the east coast of Peninsular Malaysia, off the Rompin District of Pahang. It is 20 kilometres long and 12 kilometres wide and has seven villages, of which Kampung Tekek, on the central western coast, is the largest and most populous. The island is densely forested and sparsely inhabited, and is surrounded by numerous coral reefs. Ferries reach it from the coastal towns of Mersing and Teluk Gading.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Austronesian fishermen have used Tioman for thousands of years as a navigation point and a source of fresh water and wood. During the past thousand years Chinese, Arab and European trading ships bound for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> called at the island, and shards of Chinese porcelain can often be found on its beaches. In the Second World War Tioman hosted both the British and the Japanese navies, and the waters around it are littered with war remains, among them HMS Repulse and HMS Prince of Wales.</p><p>Local tradition explains the island's shape in two ways. One legend makes it the resting place of a dragon princess who was flying to visit her prince in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, stopped in the clear waters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>, and took the form of an island to shelter passing travellers. Another says it is the dragon Sri Gumom, who was on his way to visit his sister Gunung Linga, the Lingin Peak, when Sri Rama forbade the meeting and turned him to stone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The inland rainforest was protected in 1972 as the Pulau Tioman Wildlife Reserve, although much of the original reserve was given up in 1984 for agricultural and tourist development, leaving about 8,296 hectares. The island has 45 species of mammals and 138 species of birds, and some species found nowhere else, including the Tioman walking catfish and the Kajang slender litter frog, which is known only from Mount Kajang.</p><p>The surrounding waters, with eight other nearby islands, are declared marine parks and reserves, and contain coral reefs, seagrass beds and mangrove forest. Corals were hit by a mass bleaching event in 2010. A proposal to build an international airport on reclaimed land on the west coast was rejected by the authorities in 2018 because of its environmental effects, but an environmental impact assessment for it was later submitted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tioman Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The area was absorbed into French Annam, and Mayrena died on the island of <b>Tioman</b> in 1890.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sedang">Kingdom of Sedang</a></li></ul></section>
